WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.270
Hey there, my name is Bradley Knapp, and I
am one of the product managers here at IBM

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:09.839
Cloud and we've come together today to talk
about what is a virtual server.

00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:13.250
And before we get into the video, I do want
to encourage you, please do like and subscribe

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.190
so you can get updates whenever we do new
videos like this in the future.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.250
But with that, let's go ahead and get in.

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.070
What is a virtual server?

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.630
You see it everywhere.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:23.630
Everyone talks about it.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:29.290
And if you don't know, that's OK, because
the tech press, as a rule, makes an assumption

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:34.170
that everyone has a baseline of knowledge
that is as old as theirs is.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.559
And the folks that have been in the tech press
have been there for at least 10 years.

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:38.780
They've been there for 15 years.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.200
And so, when you start out in your career
in tech, you don't know the answers to all

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.200
these questions.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.960
And there's nothing wrong with that.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.219
It's a perfectly legitimate question and it's
one that we want to help you out with.

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:57.790
So, if we think about hardware in a data center,
in your house, in your car, but physical hardware,

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.870
as a general rule, a computer is a box, right.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.170
It's a physical box.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:09.750
And inside that physical box, you're going
to have one or more CPUs, that does compute.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:16.060
You're going to have some amount of RAM that
does storage - I guess we could label these

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:22.329
CPU, RAM - and that storage is directly attached
to the CPU, the RAM is attach to the CPU,

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:23.560
so it's very close.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.270
It's for the actual operation of the machine.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.329
And then you're going to have network, right?

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:32.590
Because we're talking about virtual servers
in cloud.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.320
So, network is important.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.050
That is how the box talks to everything else.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.970
And then you're going to have traditional
storage.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:44.229
Want to put traditional storage over here
for a moment because traditional storage can

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:47.560
go inside the server, or it can be out on
its own.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:52.369
But either way, those are kind of your four
fundamental building blocks.

00:01:52.369 --> 00:02:01.359
So, a virtual server is a virtual compute,
virtual RAM, virtual network, virtual storage,

00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:05.180
but it's presented in a way that it acts like
a server does.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:10.970
So, if we consider a physical server to be
a physical device, you can put your hands

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:16.960
around, what a virtual server does is it takes
this physical host and it divides it up into

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:17.960
slices.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:18.960
Right.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.150
You've got slice one, slice two, slice three,
slice four.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:29.120
Your slices take all of the aggregate resources
on the physical server, and they present just

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.120
a little bit.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.950
Now, you don't have to do it that way.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:36.760
If instead of these a little bitty slices,
you want a great big, huge slice and then

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.379
you want to fill the box up with a little
slice at the end, that's fine, too.

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:46.130
But at the end of the day, a virtual server
is part of a physical server that you get

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.290
access to.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:52.400
And inside that virtual server, or that VSI,
you are going to have compute, you're going

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:58.220
to have RAM, you're going to have network,
and you're going to have storage.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:05.040
And it is presented to you, to the user, to
the consumer, just like a physical box would

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.040
be.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.190
But because it's virtualized, that virtualization,
what that means is that we're taking and dividing

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.500
these up, we can scale these resources up
and down.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.860
So, if you need a lot of compute in just a
little bit of RAM, that's fine, there's no

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:19.860
problem with that.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.140
We can build the virtual server that way.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.660
If you need a little bit of compute in a little
bit of RAM, but a lot of network, right.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:30.570
Like it's running a website where you're constantly
sending data out.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:31.570
That's fine.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:32.970
We can scale a virtual server that way.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.480
Each of these metrics is independently scalable.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.599
So, you can have a little bit of compute,
a little bit of RAM, a little bit of network

00:03:38.599 --> 00:03:40.860
and a ton of storage.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:46.010
That's fine because, again, a virtual server
is designed to solve whatever your specific

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:50.780
needs are, not the needs of the people who
build the box.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:53.140
And so why is this important?

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:59.489
Well, as time has gone on and very specifically
over the last 10 years, our physical server

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:00.489
boxes, right.

00:04:00.489 --> 00:04:05.560
The physical boxes that go into the data center
have gotten more and more and more powerful.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:10.799
And that power is great, but single workloads
can't take advantage of it.

00:04:10.799 --> 00:04:15.610
So, using my example before, just a normal
basic Web server, right, a normal basic web

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:25.660
server might need 4 VCPUs, 4 CPU's, it might
need 64 GBs of RAM, it might need a 1 Gbit

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:30.870
network connection and it might need, I don't
know, 250 GBs of storage.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:36.770
Well, a physical server that meets those requirements
takes up a lot of space in a data center.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:37.770
Right.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:41.000
Because our data center, if you go in there
and you've got a data center rack, right,

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:47.039
I've got my rack here, and the racks have
shelves, and the shelves are 1U tall, 2U tall,

00:04:47.039 --> 00:04:51.030
4U tall, 8U tall, you know.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:54.370
The smallest unit is a 1U.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:59.620
Now, there are 1/2-U and you can take 1U and
make it into 3 servers, but just for our purposes,

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:04.080
let's call it a physical slot in the data
center rack, 1U.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:09.419
Well, this virtual server, if we were to put
it in the physical server, would need that

00:05:09.419 --> 00:05:10.880
whole U of space.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:16.930
That's not space efficient, it's power efficient,
it's not floor print efficient, and it's bad

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:21.350
for general efficiency, not just of compute,
but it's not great for the environment either.

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:23.080
So, we want to drive density.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:28.330
So, what you do is you take that physical
box that takes up that same 1U of space, right?

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:29.330
So, 1U.

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:33.960
So, we're going to have a physical box that
takes up 1U of space, but we're going to make

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:40.360
it super powerful so that it can hold 50 of
these virtual servers because we have the

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:41.979
ability to do that.

00:05:41.979 --> 00:05:46.960
With the advances in computing technology,
we can pack 50 virtual servers of our little

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.380
Web server into 1U of space.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:50.930
This is a great thing.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:56.740
It drives down the cost because these servers,
if I can put 50 of them on one physical box,

00:05:56.740 --> 00:06:01.630
the cost for my physical box over here, I
divide up 50 ways, makes it much less expensive

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:02.650
for you.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:08.030
And it makes the compute resources that previously
you'd have to go out in a data center space,

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:09.940
get the power into it, bring the network in.

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:12.690
Now, you don't have to do that anymore.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.200
The cloud provider takes care of all of that
for you.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.680
And all you care about is that you're going
to have your one little VSI over here that's

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.979
going to cost you 20 cents an hour.

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:24.740
So that's the beauty of a virtual server.

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:25.800
That's how they work.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.889
Now, obviously, we didn't get into the great
fine technical details.

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:32.919
If you'd like to know more about that, please
do drop us a note.

00:06:32.919 --> 00:06:33.919
Let us know.

00:06:33.919 --> 00:06:36.080
We're happy to give you more information.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:37.360
But that's a virtual server.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:07:01.389
That's how they work and that's why they're
important.

